"A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation (https://arxiv.org/html/2105.11105v1)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"pattern\":\"Proposition 3.3.\"}); Total lines: 484\nL118: The crux of the algorithms of [cite12†Hit20 ] and [cite13†Har20 ] is the observation, going back to Lehman [cite21†Leh74 ], that if $N=pq$ is semiprime, then there exist “small” integers $a$ and $b$ such that $aq+bp$ is “close” to $(4abN)^{1/2}$ (see [cite13†Har20 , Lem. 3.3], or the main theorem of [cite21†Leh74 ]). In this section we prove a variant of this result in which we impose an additional constraint on $a$ and $b$ of the form $aq-bp=0\\pmod{m}$, where $m$ is a positive integer.\nL131: ###### Remark 3.2.\nL132: \nL133: Lemma cite34†3.1 can be improved slightly. Minkowski’s convex body theorem [cite35†Lan94 , §V.3, Thm. 3] implies that there exists a nonzero vector $w\\in L$ such that $|w_{1}|,|w_{2}|\\leqslant(\\det L)^{1/2}$, and such a vector can be found efficiently by a more involved algorithm; see for example [cite36†KS96 ]. This leads to better constants later in the paper, but does not affect our main asymptotic results.\nL134: ###### Proposition 3.3.\nL135: \nL136: There exists an algorithm with the following properties.\nL137: \nL138: It takes as input positive integers $N\\geqslant 2$, $m_{0}$ and $\\sigma_{0}$, a positive integer $m$ coprime to $N$, and an integer $\\sigma$ coprime to $m$ such that $1\\leqslant\\sigma\\leqslant m$.\nL139: \nL140: Its output is a pair of integers $(a,b)\\neq(0,0)$, such that if $N=pq$ is a semiprime satisfying\nL141: \nL142:  | $$\\sigma_{0}\\leqslant p<\\left(1+\\frac{1}{m_{0}}\\right)\\sigma_{0}$$  |  | (3.1)\nL143: \nL144: and\nL145: \nL146:  | $$p\\equiv\\sigma\\pmod{m},$$  |  | (3.2)\nL147: then the linear combination $aq+bp$ satisfies\nL148: \nL149:  | $\\displaystyle\\left|aq+bp-\\left(a\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}+b\\sigma_{0}\\right)\\right|\\leqslant\\frac{4N^{1/2}m^{1/2}}{m_{0}^{3/2}}$  |  | (3.3)\nL150: \nL151: and\nL152: \nL153:  | $\\displaystyle aq+bp\\equiv a\\frac{N}{\\sigma}+b\\sigma\\pmod{m^{2}}.$  |  | (3.4)\nL154: \nL155: Assuming that $m_{0},\\sigma_{0},m=O(N)$, the algorithm runs in $O(\\lg^{3}N)$ bit operations, and moreover the output satisfies $|a|,|b|=O(N^{2})$.\nL156: ###### Proof.\nL157: \nL158: Assume that the input parameters $N$, $m_{0}$, $\\sigma_{0}$, $m$ and $\\sigma$ are as described above. Assume also that $N=pq$ is a semiprime satisfying (cite37†3.1 ) and (cite38†3.2 ).\nL159: \nL160: Let $t_{0}\\coloneqq p/\\sigma_{0}-1$ so that $p=\\sigma_{0}(1+t_{0})$. Then $0\\leqslant t_{0}<1/m_{0}\\leqslant 1$, so\nL161: \nL162:  | $$q=\\frac{N}{p}=\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}(1+t_{0})^{-1}=\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}(1-t_{0}+\\delta t_{0}^{2})$$  |\nL163: \nL164: for some $\\delta\\in[0,1)$. For any pair of integers $(a,b)$, it then follows that\nL165:  | $$aq+bp=\\left(a\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}+b\\sigma_{0}\\right)+t_{0}\\left(-a\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}+b\\sigma_{0}\\right)+t_{0}^{2}\\left(\\delta a\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}\\right).$$  |  | (3.5)\nL166: \nL167: Similarly, let $t\\coloneqq p/\\sigma-1$, so that $p=\\sigma(1+t)$. Then $t$ is a rational number that is $m$-integral (i.e., its denominator is coprime to $m$), and $t\\equiv 0\\pmod{m}$. Thus\nL168: \nL169:  | $$q=\\frac{N}{p}=\\frac{N}{\\sigma}(1+t)^{-1}\\equiv\\frac{N}{\\sigma}(1-t)\\pmod{m^{2}},$$  |\nL170: \nL171: and for any pair of integers $(a,b)$ we obtain\nL172:  | $$aq+bp\\equiv\\left(a\\frac{N}{\\sigma}+b\\sigma\\right)+t\\left(-a\\frac{N}{\\sigma}+b\\sigma\\right)\\pmod{m^{2}}.$$  |\nL173: \nL174: This last congruence implies that (cite39†3.4 ) holds for any $(a,b)$ that satisfies\nL175: \nL176:  | $$-a\\frac{N}{\\sigma}+b\\sigma\\equiv 0\\pmod{m}.$$  |  | (3.6)\nL177: \nL178: If $(a,b)$ additionally satisfies the inequalities\nL179: \nL180:  | $$\\left|-a\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}+b\\sigma_{0}\\right|\\leqslant\\frac{2N^{1/2}m^{1/2}}{m_{0}^{1/2}},\\qquad\\left|a\\frac{N}{\\sigma_{0}}\\right|\\leqslant 2N^{1/2}m^{1/2}m_{0}^{1/2},$$  |  | (3.7)\nL181: then (cite40†3.5 ) implies that (cite41†3.3 ) holds. We are left with showing how to compute a pair $(a,b)\\neq(0,0)$ satisfying both (cite42†3.6 ) and (cite43†3.7 ). (The point is that both of these conditions are independent of $p$.)\nL182: The congruence (cite42†3.6 ) is equivalent to $b\\equiv\\gamma a\\pmod{m}$, where $\\gamma$ is the unique integer in $[0,m)$ congruent to $N/\\sigma^{2}$ modulo $m$. Thus the solutions $(a,b)\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ to (cite42†3.6 ) form a lattice $L=\\langle u,v\\rangle$, where $u\\coloneqq(1,\\gamma)$ and $v\\coloneqq(0,m)$. Our goal is to find a nonzero vector $(a,b)\\in L$ that lies in the parallelogram defined by (cite43†3.7 ).\nL183: \nL184: To achieve this, it is convenient to introduce the linear change of variables\nL198: ###### Remark 3.4.\nL199: \nL200: The special case $m=1$ of Proposition cite45†3.3 is closely related to [cite13†Har20 , Lem. 3.3] and the main theorem of [cite21†Leh74 ] (note that our parameter $m_{0}$ roughly corresponds to $r$ in those statements), and in fact it is possible to prove those results using a similar lattice-based approach.\nL287: 8:      Apply Proposition cite45†3.3 with input $N$, $m_{0}$, $\\sigma_{0}$, $m$ and $\\sigma$,       to obtain a pair $(a,b)=(a_{\\sigma,\\sigma_{0}},b_{\\sigma,\\sigma_{0}})$.\nL319: Let $(\\bar{\\sigma},\\bar{\\sigma}_{0})$ be the pair for which (cite37†3.1 ) and (cite38†3.2 ) hold, and consider the corresponding coefficients $\\bar{a}\\coloneqq a_{\\bar{\\sigma},\\bar{\\sigma}_{0}}$ and $\\bar{b}\\coloneqq b_{\\bar{\\sigma},\\bar{\\sigma}_{0}}$ computed in Step cite56†8 . According to Proposition cite45†3.3 , the linear combination $\\bar{a}q+\\bar{b}p$ satisfies (cite41†3.3 ) and (cite39†3.4 ) for $(\\bar{\\sigma},\\bar{\\sigma}_{0})$. Let $